  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and more humid today with more \nwidespread late day and evening showers and storms expected. \nStrong to severe storms and localized flash flooding will be \npossible.\n\nAnother active weather day across the Mid-Atlantic, though model\nguidance has trended slightly later with storm onset. The\nexpansive upper ridge over the southern tier will slowly dampen\ntoday as shortwave energy digs across the Ohio Valley. At the \nsurface, southerly return flow will push the boundary north as a\nwarm front, transporting a tropical airmass (PWATs 2.252.4\") \nback into the region.\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery shows an MCV pushing east \nacross SE OH into southwest Pennsylvania/N WV. Good agreement \nacross the past few CAM runs that these showers spread east \nthrough sunrise, potentially producing light showers over the VA\nPiedmont and northern counties by mid-morning, reaching the \nEastern Shore by early afternoon. Increased cloud cover and \nlingering showers will delay diurnal destabilization \ntemporarily. Farther to the SSW, the front is projected to push \ninto the Piedmont toward I-95 this afternoon, with MLCAPE values\nrising into the 10001500 J/kg range by mid to late afternoon.\n\nAdditional spokes of shortwave energy cross the region late \ntoday, likely triggering another round of scattered to numerous \nshowers and storms after 45 PM into this evening, with strong \nto severe storms possible. Deep-layer shear of ~2530 \nknots decent for mid-August will support multicells and bowing \nline segments capable of producing damaging wind gusts of 5060+\nmph. While less likely, all modes of severe weather remain \npossible, as strong mesoscale convergence along the warm front \nand DPVA ahead of the approaching shortwave assist in forcing \nlocally stronger updrafts. Additionally, E-SE winds along the \nwarm front and 01 km SRH values of 150200 m2/s2 will maintain \npotential rotation and an isolated tornado threat. SPC \nhighlights most of our area north of the VA/NC line in a Slight\nRisk, with a Marginal Risk for coastal Maryland and our North \nCarolina counties.\n\nAside from the severe threat, heavy rainfall is a concern as\nsteering flow gradually slows this evening. Slow-moving or back-\nbuilding storms could result in scattered instances of flash\nflooding. WPC maintains a Slight Risk ERO covering much of\ncentral and eastern VA, mainly north of US-460 and west of the\nChesapeake Bay.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid with afternoon storms Monday \nahead of the next cold front. Heat Advisories are likely to be \nneeded for Hampton Roads into Northeast NC on Monday.\n\nHot, humid conditions look to peak on Monday ahead of an\napproaching cold front, with highs reaching the low to mid 90s.\nHigh temperatures combined with dewpoints in the mid to upper\n70s will yield heat indices of 105+ across southern/southeastern\nportions of the area and 100105 elsewhere. Heat Advisories will\nlikely be needed for North Carolina counties, Hampton Roads, and\npossibly even into south-central Virginia. Some scattered \nshowers and storms are also expected Monday afternoon/evening \nalong the lingering frontal boundary. Drier and slightly cooler \nconditions arrive for Tuesday through midweek.",
